.TH "rbc::spline" 3tcl 0\&.5\&.0 "rbc" "Upgraded Tcl/Tk9\&.0-ready RBC package"
.SH NAME
rbc::spline - Commands in namespace rbc::spline
.SH "SPLINE INTERPOLATION"
.PP
The spline command performs spline interpolation and extrapolation on data stored in Rbc vectors\&. Two
interpolation methods are available:
.TP
\(bu
natural - natural cubic spline interpolation\&.
.TP
\(bu
quadratic - shape-preserving quadratic spline interpolation\&.
.PP
Both operations use the same four-vector interface:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
spline method x y splX splY
.fi
.RE
.PP
The vectors x and y contain the source data points\&. The vector splX contains the X coordinates at
which the spline is evaluated, and splY receives the corresponding calculated Y coordinates\&.
.SS "SOURCE DATA"
.PP
The source vectors x and y must have the same length and contain at least three values\&. All source
X and Y values must be finite\&.
.PP
Values in x must be strictly increasing\&. Duplicate or decreasing X coordinates are not permitted\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
vector create x y

x set {0 1 2 3 4}
y set {0 2 1 4 3}
.fi
.RE
.PP
If source data are not already ordered by X coordinate, the vector sort operation can reorder x while
applying the same permutation to y:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x sort y
.fi
.RE
.SS "EVALUATION COORDINATES"
.PP
The splX vector specifies the X coordinates at which the spline is evaluated\&. All values in splX must
be finite\&.
.PP
Unlike the source x vector, splX does not have to be sorted\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any
order, and the values in splY are produced in the corresponding order\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
splX set {2\&.5 0\&.5 4\&.5 -1\&.0 1\&.5}
spline natural x y splX splY
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each component of splY corresponds to the component at the same position in splX\&.
.PP
Evaluation coordinates may lie outside the range of the source x vector\&. Both spline methods extrapolate
such points:
.TP
\(bu
values below the first source X coordinate are extrapolated using the first spline interval;
.TP
\(bu
values above the last source X coordinate are extrapolated using the last spline interval\&.
.PP
Thus interpolation and extrapolation use the same command and no separate extrapolation option is required\&.
.SS "GENERATING EVALUATION COORDINATES"
.PP
The vector populate operation is convenient for generating a more densely sampled set of X coordinates
between the original source points:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x populate splX 10
spline natural x y splX splY
.fi
.RE
.PP
populate is not required\&. splX may contain any finite coordinates appropriate for the application\&.
.SS "OUTPUT VECTOR"
.PP
The result vector splY has the same length as splX\&.
.PP
If splY does not exist, it is created\&. If it already exists, it is resized when necessary and its previous
contents are replaced\&.
.PP
Spline generation is transactional\&. The source vectors and evaluation coordinates are validated and the
complete result is calculated before splY is created, resized, or modified\&. If spline generation fails,
an existing splY vector is left unchanged\&.
.PP
Because the inputs are copied before the output vector is modified, splY may safely be the same vector as
x, y, or splX\&.
.SS EXAMPLE
.PP
The following example generates both natural and quadratic spline values from the same source data:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
vector create x y splX

x set {0 1 2 3 4}
y set {0 2 1 4 3}

splX set {-1\&.0 0\&.0 0\&.5 1\&.5 2\&.5 4\&.0 5\&.0}

spline natural x y splX naturalY
spline quadratic x y splX quadraticY
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first and last evaluation coordinates lie outside the source range and are therefore extrapolated\&.
.SS "NATURAL CUBIC SPLINE"
.PP
A natural spline is a piecewise cubic interpolating function that passes through every source data point\&.
Its second derivative is zero at the first and last source points, providing the natural boundary
conditions\&.
.PP
For evaluation coordinates outside the source range, the cubic polynomial of the first or last spline
interval is continued beyond its endpoint\&.
.PP
Natural cubic splines generally produce smooth curves with continuous first and second derivatives\&.
.SS "SHAPE-PRESERVING QUADRATIC SPLINE"
.PP
The quadratic method generates an osculatory piecewise quadratic spline\&. Slopes at the source points are
selected to preserve monotonicity and convexity where those properties are compatible with the source data\&.
.PP
For evaluation coordinates outside the source range, the spline defined by the first or last source
interval is continued beyond its endpoint\&.
.PP
This method can be useful when preserving the qualitative shape of the source data is more important than
the additional smoothness of a cubic spline\&.
.SS ERRORS
.PP
Spline generation fails if:
.TP
\(bu
either source vector contains fewer than three values;
.TP
\(bu
the source vectors have different lengths;
.TP
\(bu
a source X or Y value is not finite;
.TP
\(bu
the source X coordinates are not strictly increasing;
.TP
\(bu
an evaluation X coordinate is not finite;
.TP
\(bu
required temporary storage cannot be allocated;
.TP
\(bu
the spline calculation produces an invalid numerical result\&.
.PP
An error does not partially modify the output vector\&.
.SS REFERENCES
.PP
The natural cubic spline implementation is based on:
.TP
\(bu
R\&. L\&. Burden, J\&. D\&. Faires and A\&. C\&. Reynolds, \fINumerical Analysis\fP, Prindle, Weber & Schmidt, 1981\&.
.PP
The shape-preserving quadratic spline implementation is based on:
.TP
\(bu
D\&. F\&. McAllister and J\&. A\&. Roulier, \fIAn Algorithm for Computing a Shape-Preserving Osculatory Quadratic Spline\fP, ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software, Algorithm 574\&.

